            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings>

There is currently 6" (R20) of attic insulation. I have modeled the opportunity to increase this up to a minimum 15" (R50).



Savings: By increasing the attic insulation from R20 to R50, this is estimated to save $ 202 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.1 GJ, and qualify for a $ 375 rebate through the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</ceilings>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a power vented hot water tank, that draws its' combustion air from inside the house. There is a 4" combustion air supply pipe attached to the side of the furnace, and this combustion air pipe is supplying air for both the hot water tank and the wood pellet stove to use. With the air tightness of your home, I have modeled an option to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 393 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Pellet Stove Combustion Air &amp; Circulation



You have an Enviro wood pellet stove that has the opportunity to have an outside combustion air supply directly connected to the back of the stove, however this is not currently installed. With your house being quite air tight, the wood pellet stove relies on the 4" combustion air supply pipe in the furnace room (that also supplies combustion air for the hot water tank). I would suggest you have the dedicated combustion air supply pipe installed for the wood pellet stove.



For circulation of the wood pellet stove heat throughout the house, you could use the furnace circulation fan to help accomplish this. Having a return air supply through the basement partition wall that adjoins the room where the pellet stove is would work well. I saw 2 houses in Hay River in July where they had a separate thermostat in the basement wired into their furnace that was programmed to turn on the furnace fan only whenever the basement temperature (from the wood or pellet stoves) reached a programmed high temperature.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - a range hood fan (that is currently not venting out - however you should set it up to vent out)

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



Your house is currently very air tight and NRCan's modeling software indicates the house is under ventilated. If the house was being built today, it would have an HRV installed, however retrofitting one in now would be difficult with a fully finished basement.



There is a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace and I did not see a manual damper on it (to open or close it), so I am assuming it is in the fully open position. Every time the furnace operates, this fresh air pipe will bring in some outside fresh air and distribute it throughout the house via the heating duct distribution system.



The 5" fresh air pipe also operates as a "makeup" air supply to your house whenever an exhaust fan is on (ie: one of the 2 bathroom fans or the dryer). We identified that the kitchen range hood fan was not venting outside, however it appears there is a vent up through the roof for it, so I think the range hood fan just needs to be re-installed so that it is connected to vent outside.



For the under ventilation of your house, you may be able to manage it by one of the following techniques:

- have the main floor bathroom fan set up with a timer switch or with an integrated humidistat

- connect the range hood fan to vent outside, and use it while cooking

- install an HRV connected to the supply &amp; return ductwork of your furnace and have the HRV wired into the furnace to turn on the furnace fan each time the HRV cycles on.



Personally, I would try the first 2 options above before proceeding to installing an HRV.



NOTE: Increasing ventilation in the house is expected to cost you an additional $60 per year in combined heating &amp; electrical use, however having proper ventilation is definitely worth it !



For the main floor bathroom, you may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (both bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.4 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current gas hot water tank and the pellet stove draw their combustion air from inside the house, however both appliance are "power vented" and will usually not back-draft combustion gas until a negative pressure of 15 Pascals is created. Once the kitchen range hood exhaust is connected to vent outside, your house may reach 10 - 15 Pascals of negative pressure potential.



During my assessment of your home, I did see combination smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.18 per litre 

Propane - $2.42/m3 community piped system, $0.79 per litre delivered

Wood - $289 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.73 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climah transportation costs, and climate change!

